{"edits":[{"content":"The proposal under review introduces a carriage-closure rule that adds edges from any attacker of a carrier to the warrant it carries, asserting that the existing fixed point remains intact and all guarantees carry over verbatim. However, the objection is that this closure is not guaranteed to be confluent when carriage chains exist. For instance, if artifact a1 carries warrant w1 that names target t, and artifact a2 carries warrant w2 that names a1, then attackers of a2 become attackers of w2, and w2 attacks a1, potentially exposing a1 to new self-referential cycles through w1. The sequence in which closure edges are inserted could affect whether a self-attack on a carrier appears before its warrant edges are resolved, making the final edge set dependent on order unless confluence is proven. The proposal itself acknowledges that closure might create self-attack cycles that invalidate previously accepted carriers, meaning reinstatement-as-derived and root validity could fail for those carriers.\n\nThe rebuttal argues that the critic is mixing an algorithmic process (sequential edge addition) with a mathematical definition (the closure of a relation). The proposal defines carriage-closure as an invariant that expands the edge set seen by the grounded semantics; it is not an iterative procedure with order-dependent output. The critic's claim of order-dependence rests on the assumption that edge addition order matters, but a relational closure is defined as the unique minimal superset satisfying the closure condition—a set defined by a fixed-point property, not a sequence. The proposal states closure is a deterministic, one-pass, linear-time expansion of the attack relation. The critic has not shown that chained carriage fails to have a least fixed point; they merely assert non-confluence without identifying a property of the carriage rule that prevents uniqueness. The burden is on the critic to demonstrate that the closure operator is ill-defined, which they have not done. Regarding self-attack cycles, the critic's characterization of the proposal's admission is inaccurate: the proposal identifies that claim as the one to be defended, not as a concession. The critic must independently prove that such cycles arise and that they newly refute previously accepted carriers. The scenario described (a1 carries w1, a2 carries w2 naming a1, attackers of a2 become attackers of w2, w2 attacks a1) does not establish a self-attack on a1; that would require an edge from a1 to itself, which the critic has not shown the closure produces. The attacks described are ordinary attack edges, not self-attacks. Finally, the critic's assertion that reinstatement-as-derived and root validity can fail lacks any argument beyond the unestablished self-attack claim. If closure is a well-defined deterministic expansion, the grounded semantics operates on the expanded relation identically, and the existing proofs transfer because the algorithm is unchanged. The critic has not pinpointed any step in those proofs that breaks when the input is the closure rather than the original relation. In summary, the critic fails to establish non-confluence, fails to demonstrate an actual self-attack cycle, and fails to show any proof obligation that fails; the argument relies on hypothetical order-dependence that does not apply to a single deterministic closure computation."},{"content":"The examined modification introduces a carriage-closure operation that appends edges from every attacker of a carrier to the warrant it conveys, and it asserts that the pre-existing fixed point remains unaltered, with all guarantees preserved exactly. A critic counters that this closure may not be confluent under chained carriage: suppose a1 carries w1 which names t, and a2 carries w2 which names a1; then a2's attackers also attack w2, and w2 attacks a1, which could newly place a1 into self-attacking cycles via w1. The sequence of edge additions could determine whether a self-attack on a carrier shows up before the carrier's warrant edges are resolved, so the final edge set is order-dependent unless confluence is proven. The proposal also admits that closure might generate self-attack cycles that newly undermine previously accepted carriers, which would break reinstatement-as-derived and root validity for those carriers.\n\nThe response to the critic points out a confusion between an algorithmic procedure (ordered edge insertion) and a mathematical construct (the closure of a relation). The proposal treats carriage-closure as an explicit invariant that expands the edge set for the grounded semantics, not as a step-by-step process whose outcome varies with processing order. The critic's entire order-dependence argument assumes that the order of adding edges matters, but a relational closure is the unique smallest superset of the original relation that satisfies the closure rule—a set defined by a fixed-point condition, not by a sequence of additions. The proposal says closure is a deterministic, linear-time expansion of the attack relation, meaning the expansion function computes the full closure in a single pass. The critic has not demonstrated that chained-carriage closure lacks a unique least fixed point; they simply assert that 'closure is not necessarily confluent' without pointing to any specific feature of the carriage rule that would prevent uniqueness. The burden lies with the critic to show that the closure operator is not well-defined, and no such proof is given. As for self-attack cycles, the critic's claim that the proposal 'explicitly admits' they can be manufactured is a misreading: the proposal names that as the claim to be defended, not as an admission. The critic must independently show that such cycles occur and that they newly refute previously accepted carriers. The described scenario—where a1 carries w1, a2 carries w2 naming a1, attackers of a2 attack w2, and w2 attacks a1—does not by itself show a1 is 'newly exposed to self-attacking cycles through w1.' A self-attack on a1 would require an edge from a1 to itself, which the critic has not proven the closure rule produces; the edges described are ordinary attacks, not self-attacks. Regarding reinstatement-as-derived and root validity, the critic asserts they 'can fail' but provides no reasoning beyond the unsubstantiated self-attack claim. If closure is a well-defined deterministic expansion, the grounded semantics works on the expanded relation exactly as before, and the proofs from the unexpanded case transfer because the algorithm is unchanged. The critic has not identified any step in the existing proofs that breaks when the input is the closure rather than the original relation. In conclusion, the critic has not established non-confluence, has not shown an actual self-attack cycle, and has not demonstrated any failing proof obligation; the entire case rests on hypothetical assertions about order-dependence that are irrelevant to a relational closure computed as a single deterministic expansion."}]}